The simplest modern way is flexbox on the parent:

```css
.parent {
  display: flex;
  justify-content: center; /* horizontal */
  align-items: center;     /* vertical */
  min-height: 100vh;
}
```

Grid works too: `display: grid; place-items: center;`. The parent needs a height (here `min-height`) or there is nothing to center within.

Flex items stay on one line by default. Add `flex-wrap: wrap` to the container. If a child still overflows, it is probably wider than the container because of a fixed `width` or long unbroken text; set `min-width: 0` on the flex child (so it may shrink below its content size) and `overflow-wrap: anywhere` for long strings.

```css
.row { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 12px; }
.row > .item { flex: 1 1 200px; min-width: 0; }
```

Lean on fluid techniques first: relative units, `clamp()` for type, and `auto-fit`/`minmax` grids that reflow on their own.

```css
.cards { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 260px), 1fr)); gap: 1rem; }
h1 { font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 4vw, 2.5rem); }
img { max-width: 100%; height: auto; }
```

Add a media query only where the design really needs to change (for example, switching a sidebar to a top bar). Don't forget `<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">`.

Define colours as custom properties and override them in a media query:

```css
:root { --bg: #ffffff; --fg: #1a1a1a; }
@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
  :root { --bg: #121212; --fg: #eaeaea; }
}
body { background: var(--bg); color: var(--fg); }
```

If you also offer a manual toggle, set a `data-theme` attribute and put the overrides under `:root[data-theme="dark"]` as well.

Wrap it in a container that scrolls horizontally, which keeps the table semantics:

```css
.table-wrap { overflow-x: auto; }
.table-wrap table { min-width: 600px; }
```

For a few columns, you can instead reflow rows into stacked cards at small widths with CSS, but keep real table markup for screen readers. Hide low-priority columns only if the information is not essential.

My counter in React only goes up by 1 even though I call setCount(count + 1) three times:

```jsx
function add3() {
  setCount(count + 1);
  setCount(count + 1);
  setCount(count + 1);
}
```

Each call reads the same `count` from the current render, so all three schedule `count + 1` and you get +1. Use the functional form, which receives the latest pending state:

```jsx
function add3() {
  setCount(c => c + 1);
  setCount(c => c + 1);
  setCount(c => c + 1);
}
```

That yields +3. Use the updater form whenever the new state depends on the previous one.

Why does my setInterval in useEffect always log the old state?

```jsx
useEffect(() => {
  const id = setInterval(() => console.log(count), 1000);
  return () => clearInterval(id);
}, []);
```

My useEffect causes an infinite loop:

```jsx
useEffect(() => {
  setItems([...items, 'x']);
}, [items]);
```

The effect depends on `items` and also changes `items`, so every run triggers another render and another run. Ask what should trigger the update. If it should happen once, use `[]` with the updater form:

```jsx
useEffect(() => {
  setItems(prev => [...prev, 'x']);
}, []);
```

If the value is derived from other state, compute it during render instead of storing it in state plus an effect.

Do I need useEffect to fetch data? Is this OK?

```jsx
useEffect(() => {
  fetch(`/api/users/${id}`).then(r => r.json()).then(setUser);
}, [id]);
```

It works but has two problems. If `id` changes quickly, responses can arrive out of order and an old response overwrites the newer one. There is also no error or loading handling. Cancel the stale request:

```jsx
useEffect(() => {
  const ctrl = new AbortController();
  fetch(`/api/users/${id}`, { signal: ctrl.signal })
    .then(r => { if (!r.ok) throw new Error(r.status); return r.json(); })
    .then(setUser)
    .catch(e => { if (e.name !== 'AbortError') setError(e); });
  return () => ctrl.abort();
}, [id]);
```

For real apps, a data-fetching library (such as TanStack Query) gives caching, retries and deduplication so you write far less of this by hand.

A controlled input gets its value from state and reports changes through a handler, so React is the source of truth:

```jsx
function Name() {
  const [name, setName] = useState('');
  return <input value={name} onChange={e => setName(e.target.value)} />;
}
```

If you set `value` without `onChange`, the field becomes read-only and React warns. Use `defaultValue` for an uncontrolled input whose value you read on submit (with a ref or `FormData`).

Lift it to their closest common parent and pass it down as props, with callbacks for changes:

```jsx
function Parent() {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');
  return (
    <>
      <SearchBox value={query} onChange={setQuery} />
      <Results query={query} />
    </>
  );
}
```

If the state must travel through many layers, consider context or a state library, but start with lifting; it is the simplest option and keeps data flow explicit.

Why does my memoized child still re-render?

```jsx
const Child = React.memo(function Child({ onSave }) { ... });
function Parent() {
  return <Child onSave={() => save()} />;
}
```

`React.memo` compares props shallowly, and the arrow function `() => save()` is a new function on every render of `Parent`, so the prop is always "different". Stabilise it with `useCallback`:

```jsx
const onSave = useCallback(() => save(), []);
return <Child onSave={onSave} />;
```

List every value the function uses in the dependency array. The same applies to inline objects and arrays passed as props.

Is it a problem that I mutate state directly?

```jsx
const [todos, setTodos] = useState([]);
function add(t) {
  todos.push(t);
  setTodos(todos);
}
```

Use `useState` for simple, independent values. Switch to `useReducer` when state has several related fields, when the next state depends on complex rules, or when many different actions update it. A reducer centralises the transitions in a pure function that is easy to test:

```jsx
function reducer(state, action) {
  switch (action.type) {
    case 'add': return { ...state, items: [...state.items, action.item] };
    case 'clear': return { ...state, items: [] };
    default: return state;
  }
}
const [state, dispatch] = useReducer(reducer, { items: [] });
```

No. Hooks must be called in the same order on every render, at the top level of a component or custom hook, not inside conditions, loops or nested functions. React tracks hooks by call order, so a skipped call corrupts the state mapping. Put the condition inside the hook instead:

```jsx
useEffect(() => {
  if (!enabled) return;
  // ...
}, [enabled]);
```

A custom hook is a function whose name starts with `use` and which calls other hooks:

```jsx
function useDebounced(value, ms) {
  const [debounced, setDebounced] = useState(value);
  useEffect(() => {
    const id = setTimeout(() => setDebounced(value), ms);
    return () => clearTimeout(id);
  }, [value, ms]);
  return debounced;
}
```

Use it as `const q = useDebounced(input, 300);` and fetch when `q` changes. The cleanup function cancels the previous timer, which is what makes it a debounce.

Handle `onSubmit` on the `<form>` and call `preventDefault()`:

```jsx
function Login() {
  const [email, setEmail] = useState('');
  async function onSubmit(e) {
    e.preventDefault();
    await fetch('/api/login', {
      method: 'POST',
      headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
      body: JSON.stringify({ email }),
    });
  }
  return (
    <form onSubmit={onSubmit}>
      <input type="email" value={email} onChange={e => setEmail(e.target.value)} required />
      <button type="submit">Sign in</button>
    </form>
  );
}
```

Submitting via the form (not a button `onClick`) also makes the Enter key work. Never put a password in the URL or log it.

Test behaviour from the user's point of view with React Testing Library: render the component, interact as a user would, and assert on what is visible.

```jsx
import { render, screen } from '@testing-library/react';
import userEvent from '@testing-library/user-event';

test('adds a todo', async () => {
  render(<Todos />);
  await userEvent.type(screen.getByLabelText(/new todo/i), 'milk');
  await userEvent.click(screen.getByRole('button', { name: /add/i }));
  expect(screen.getByText('milk')).toBeInTheDocument();
});
```

Query by role and label, which also encourages accessible markup, and avoid testing internal state or implementation details.

Review this component:

```jsx
function Profile({ userId }) {
  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);
  fetch(`/api/users/${userId}`).then(r => r.json()).then(setUser);
  return <div>{user.name}</div>;
}
```

Several problems:

1. The `fetch` runs during render, so it fires on every render, and `setUser` triggers another render: an infinite request loop.
2. `user` is `null` on the first render, so `user.name` throws.
3. No error or loading state.

Move the request into an effect keyed on `userId`, and render defensively:

```jsx
function Profile({ userId }) {
  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);
  useEffect(() => {
    let cancelled = false;
    fetch(`/api/users/${userId}`)
      .then(r => r.json())
      .then(u => { if (!cancelled) setUser(u); });
    return () => { cancelled = true; };
  }, [userId]);
  if (!user) return <p>Loading...</p>;
  return <div>{user.name}</div>;
}
```

For many fields, an uncontrolled approach avoids re-rendering the whole form on every keystroke. Use a form library (such as React Hook Form) or read values from `FormData` on submit:

```jsx
function onSubmit(e) {
  e.preventDefault();
  const data = Object.fromEntries(new FormData(e.currentTarget));
  save(data);
}
```

Use controlled inputs where you need live validation or dependent fields. Always validate again on the server.

It runs before the effect re-runs and when the component unmounts. Use it to undo what the effect set up: remove event listeners, clear timers, abort requests, close sockets, unsubscribe.

```jsx
useEffect(() => {
  const onResize = () => setWidth(window.innerWidth);
  window.addEventListener('resize', onResize);
  return () => window.removeEventListener('resize', onResize);
}, []);
```

Without the cleanup, each mount adds another listener and you leak memory.

```python
from fastapi import FastAPI

app = FastAPI()

@app.get("/items/{item_id}")
def read_item(item_id: int, verbose: bool = False):
    return {"id": item_id, "verbose": verbose}
```

`item_id` appears in the path, so FastAPI treats it as a path parameter and validates it as an integer (a non-integer returns a 422 response). `verbose` is not in the path, so it becomes an optional query parameter: `/items/5?verbose=true`.

Declare a Pydantic model and use it as the parameter type:

```python
from fastapi import FastAPI
from pydantic import BaseModel, Field

app = FastAPI()

class TaskIn(BaseModel):
    title: str = Field(min_length=1, max_length=120)
    priority: int = Field(default=3, ge=1, le=5)

@app.post("/tasks", status_code=201)
def create_task(task: TaskIn):
    return {"title": task.title, "priority": task.priority}
```

Invalid bodies are rejected automatically with a 422 response listing which fields failed. Keep separate input and output models so clients can't set fields like `id` or `is_admin`.

`Depends` declares a dependency: a callable FastAPI runs for you and whose result it passes into your endpoint. It's used for database sessions, authentication, pagination parameters and so on, and dependencies can have their own dependencies.

```python
from fastapi import Depends, FastAPI

app = FastAPI()

def pagination(skip: int = 0, limit: int = 20):
    return {"skip": skip, "limit": min(limit, 100)}

@app.get("/users")
def list_users(page: dict = Depends(pagination)):
    return page
```

In tests you can override a dependency (`app.dependency_overrides[...]`) to inject a fake database.

Declare a response model that lists only the safe fields; FastAPI filters the output to it:

```python
from pydantic import BaseModel, ConfigDict

class UserOut(BaseModel):
    model_config = ConfigDict(from_attributes=True)
    id: int
    email: str

@app.get("/users/{user_id}", response_model=UserOut)
def get_user(user_id: int, db=Depends(get_db)):
    return db.get(User, user_id)
```

`password_hash` isn't in `UserOut`, so it's never serialised. Having separate input, output and database models is a defence against accidentally exposing columns later.

Use `BackgroundTasks` for short, best-effort work:

```python
from fastapi import BackgroundTasks, FastAPI

app = FastAPI()

def send_welcome(email: str):
    ...  # your email code

@app.post("/signup", status_code=201)
def signup(email: str, background: BackgroundTasks):
    background.add_task(send_welcome, email)
    return {"status": "created"}
```

The task runs in the same process after the response is sent, so it is lost if the process dies. For work that must not be lost, or that is heavy or needs retries, use a proper job queue and worker.

Write a central error-handling middleware with four parameters, registered after your routes, and forward errors to it with `next(err)`:

```javascript
app.get('/users/:id', async (req, res, next) => {
  try {
    const user = await findUser(req.params.id);
    if (!user) return res.status(404).json({ error: 'not_found' });
    res.json(user);
  } catch (err) {
    next(err);
  }
});

app.use((err, req, res, next) => {
  console.error(err);
  res.status(500).json({ error: 'internal_error' });
});
```

In Express 4, errors thrown in async handlers aren't caught automatically, hence the `try/catch` (Express 5 handles rejected promises for you). Don't send stack traces to clients.

Never trust `req.body`, `req.query` or `req.params`. Validate shape, types and ranges at the edge with a schema library (such as Zod or Joi) and reject bad input with 400/422 before it reaches business logic:

```javascript
const { z } = require('zod');
const Task = z.object({ title: z.string().min(1).max(120), priority: z.number().int().min(1).max(5).default(3) });

app.post('/tasks', (req, res) => {
  const parsed = Task.safeParse(req.body);
  if (!parsed.success) return res.status(422).json({ error: 'invalid', details: parsed.error.issues });
  res.status(201).json(parsed.data);
});
```

Validation also strips unexpected fields, which helps prevent mass-assignment bugs.

It works, but scattering it makes misconfiguration hard to catch. Read and validate all environment variables in one config module at startup, and fail fast if one is missing:

```javascript
function required(name) {
  const v = process.env[name];
  if (!v) throw new Error(`Missing required env var ${name}`);
  return v;
}
module.exports = { jwtSecret: required('JWT_SECRET'), port: Number(process.env.PORT ?? 3000) };
```

Never commit `.env` files or log their values.

Be consistent and machine-readable: a status code that matches the problem, a stable error code clients can switch on, a human message, and field details for validation errors.

```json
{
  "error": {
    "code": "validation_failed",
    "message": "One or more fields are invalid.",
    "fields": [{"field": "email", "issue": "must be a valid address"}]
  }
}
```

Don't leak stack traces, SQL or internal paths. Some teams adopt the standard Problem Details format (RFC 9457) for the same purpose.

TypeScript says 'Object is possibly undefined' on this line:

```typescript
const el = document.getElementById('app');
el.innerHTML = 'hi';
```

`getElementById` returns `HTMLElement | null`, because the element may not exist, and with `strictNullChecks` you must handle that. Prefer a check over a non-null assertion:

```typescript
const el = document.getElementById('app');
if (!el) throw new Error('#app not found');
el.innerHTML = 'hi';
```

`el!.innerHTML` silences the error but will throw at runtime if the element is missing.

Generics let one function or type work over many types while keeping them connected.

```typescript
function groupBy<T, K extends string | number>(items: T[], key: (item: T) => K): Record<K, T[]> {
  const out = {} as Record<K, T[]>;
  for (const item of items) {
    const k = key(item);
    (out[k] ??= []).push(item);
  }
  return out;
}

const byDept = groupBy([{ name: 'Ana', dept: 'eng' }, { name: 'Bo', dept: 'ops' }], (p) => p.dept);
```

`T` is inferred as the element type, so `byDept.eng` is typed as an array of those objects, with no `any`.

Use overloads, or a discriminated union for the result.

```typescript
type Result<T> = { ok: true; value: T } | { ok: false; error: string };

function parsePort(s: string): Result<number> {
  const n = Number(s);
  return Number.isInteger(n) && n > 0 && n < 65536 ? { ok: true, value: n } : { ok: false, error: 'invalid port' };
}

const r = parsePort('8080');
if (r.ok) console.log(r.value); else console.error(r.error);
```

The `ok` field lets the compiler narrow the type in each branch, so callers can't read `value` without checking.

Review this login code for security issues:

```python
@app.post("/login")
def login(email: str, password: str):
    user = db.query(User).filter(User.email == email).first()
    if user is None:
        return {"error": "No such user"}
    if user.password != password:
        return {"error": "Wrong password"}
    return {"token": str(user.id)}
```

Several serious problems:

1. **Plain-text password comparison**: passwords must be stored as salted slow hashes (bcrypt, argon2) and verified with the library.
2. **User enumeration**: different messages for unknown user and wrong password reveal which emails exist. Return one generic message.
3. **The token is the user id**: anyone can guess it. Issue a random session token or a signed, expiring token.
4. **Credentials as query parameters**: `email` and `password` declared like this come from the URL query string; they should be in the POST body over HTTPS, since URLs end up in logs.
5. **No rate limiting or lockout**, so brute force is easy.

```python
GENERIC = {"error": "Invalid email or password"}

@app.post("/login")
def login(body: LoginIn):
    user = get_user_by_email(body.email)
    if user is None or not verify_password(body.password, user.password_hash):
        raise HTTPException(401, GENERIC["error"])
    return {"token": create_session_token(user.id)}
```

Add a proxy to `vite.config.js` so `/api` goes to the backend:

```javascript
export default {
  server: { proxy: { '/api': 'http://localhost:8000' } },
};
```

Restart the dev server. In production, make your reverse proxy route `/api` to the backend before the fallback to `index.html`.

Two bugs: the response key is `results`, not `items`, and the first render happens before the fetch completes. Guard the render and use the right field:

```jsx
if (!data) return <p>Loading...</p>;
return data.results.map(r => <Row key={r.id} row={r} />);
```

If the shape is uncertain, log `console.log(data)` once, or validate the response before putting it in state.

What's the output of this Node.js code, and why?

```javascript
const results = [];
for (const delay of [30, 10, 20]) {
  setTimeout(() => results.push(delay), delay);
}
setTimeout(() => console.log(results.join(",")), 50);
```

Does this async code run the two requests in parallel or one after another?

```javascript
const wait = (ms) => new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, ms));
(async () => {
  const t0 = Date.now();
  await wait(60);
  await wait(60);
  const seq = Date.now() - t0 >= 110;
  const t1 = Date.now();
  await Promise.all([wait(60), wait(60)]);
  const par = Date.now() - t1 < 110;
  console.log("sequential takes about the sum:", seq, "| parallel takes about the max:", par);
})();
```

How do I parse a query string safely in Node?

```javascript
const url = new URL("https://example.com/search?q=node+js&page=2&tag=a&tag=b");
console.log(url.searchParams.get("q"));
console.log(Number(url.searchParams.get("page")));
console.log(url.searchParams.getAll("tag"));
```

How do I read JSON from a string and handle bad input?

```javascript
function parse(text) {
  try {
    return { ok: true, value: JSON.parse(text) };
  } catch (e) {
    return { ok: false, error: e.name };
  }
}
console.log(JSON.stringify(parse('{"a": 1}')));
console.log(JSON.stringify(parse("{a: 1}")));
```

`JSON.parse` throws on invalid input, so wrap it and return a result instead of letting the exception escape a request handler. Output:

```
{"ok":true,"value":{"a":1}}
{"ok":false,"error":"SyntaxError"}
```

Parsing only proves the text is JSON; you still need to validate that the value has the fields and types you expect.

How do I make a simple in-memory cache with expiry in Node?

```javascript
class TtlCache {
  constructor(ttlMs) { this.ttl = ttlMs; this.map = new Map(); }
  set(k, v) { this.map.set(k, { v, exp: Date.now() + this.ttl }); }
  get(k) {
    const e = this.map.get(k);
    if (!e) return undefined;
    if (e.exp < Date.now()) { this.map.delete(k); return undefined; }
    return e.v;
  }
}
const c = new TtlCache(30);
c.set("a", 1);
console.log(c.get("a"));
setTimeout(() => console.log(c.get("a")), 60);
```

Why does this loop block the server even though I used async/await?

```javascript
let ticks = 0;
const timer = setInterval(() => ticks++, 10);
(async () => {
  const end = Date.now() + 100;
  while (Date.now() < end) {}          // synchronous busy loop inside an async function
  console.log("ticks during busy loop:", ticks);
  await new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, 55));
  console.log("ticks after yielding:", ticks > 0);
  clearInterval(timer);
})();
```

How can I create a unique, safe filename for an upload in Node?

```javascript
const crypto = require("node:crypto");
const path = require("node:path");
function storedName(original) {
  const ext = path.extname(original).toLowerCase();
  const allowed = new Set([".png", ".jpg", ".pdf"]);
  if (!allowed.has(ext)) throw new Error("type not allowed");
  return crypto.randomUUID() + ext;
}
const n = storedName("../../My Photo.PNG");
console.log(/^[0-9a-f-]{36}\.png$/.test(n));
try { storedName("run.exe"); } catch (e) { console.log(e.message); }
```

Animate properties the browser can handle on the compositor: `transform` and `opacity`. Animating `width`, `height`, `top` or `left` triggers layout on every frame. Avoid layout-triggering work during the animation, use `will-change` sparingly, and respect users who prefer less motion:

```css
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  * { animation: none !important; transition: none !important; }
}
```

Define the type you expect, then validate the data against it before trusting it. A hand-written guard works for small shapes:

```typescript
interface User { id: number; name: string }

function isUser(x: unknown): x is User {
  return typeof x === 'object' && x !== null
    && typeof (x as any).id === 'number'
    && typeof (x as any).name === 'string';
}

const data: unknown = await res.json();
if (!isUser(data)) throw new Error('Unexpected response shape');
```

For larger APIs, generate types and validators from an OpenAPI spec or a schema library, so the checks and types come from one source.

Can you review this Express route?

```javascript
app.get('/files', (req, res) => {
  const name = req.query.name;
  res.sendFile('/srv/data/' + name);
});
```

This is a path traversal vulnerability: a request like `?name=../../etc/passwd` escapes `/srv/data`. Also nothing checks that `name` exists or is a string. Resolve the path and check it stays inside the base directory, or use `sendFile`'s `root` option, and validate the name:

```javascript
const path = require('node:path');

app.get('/files', (req, res) => {
  const name = String(req.query.name || '');
  if (!/^[\w.-]+$/.test(name)) return res.status(400).json({ error: 'bad_name' });
  res.sendFile(name, { root: '/srv/data' }, (err) => {
    if (err) res.status(404).json({ error: 'not_found' });
  });
});
```

An allow-list of file names is even safer if the set is known.

Is there a problem with this retry logic?

```javascript
async function getWithRetry(url) {
  while (true) {
    try {
      return await fetch(url);
    } catch (e) {}
  }
}
```

Yes: it retries forever, with no delay and no limit, and swallows every error. If the service is down it spins in a hot loop, flooding it (and your logs are silent). Bound the attempts, back off between tries, and surface the final error:

```javascript
async function getWithRetry(url, tries = 4) {
  for (let i = 0; i < tries; i++) {
    try {
      const res = await fetch(url);
      if (res.status < 500) return res;   // don't retry client errors
    } catch (e) {
      if (i === tries - 1) throw e;
    }
    await new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, 200 * 2 ** i));
  }
  throw new Error('service unavailable after retries');
}
```

Only retry operations that are safe to repeat.

Not if `userInput` is arbitrary: characters like `&`, `#`, `=` or spaces change the meaning of the URL (parameter injection) or break it. Build URLs with the `URL` and `URLSearchParams` classes, which encode values for you:

```javascript
const url = new URL('https://api.example.com/search');
url.searchParams.set('q', userInput);
url.searchParams.set('limit', '10');
```

Use `encodeURIComponent` if you must build a string manually.

Review this CSS:

```css
.btn { width: 100px; height: 30px; font-size: 11px; color: #aaa; background: #eee; }
.btn:focus { outline: none; }
```
